    "engine": "julia",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Integrals\"\nengine: julia\n---\n\n\n\n\nA negative `derivative` order gives an interpolant of an integral: `derivative = -1` is the\nantiderivative, `-2` the twofold integral, and so on. Like the derivatives, integrals are\nevaluated analytically from the kernel's polynomial pieces, not by quadrature, and they are\nanchored at the first knot: there the integral is zero, and so are all lower integrals. So the\nantiderivative evaluated at $b$ is $\\int_{x_0}^{b}$ of the interpolant, and a definite integral\nbetween two limits is the difference of two evaluations.\n\n## A first example\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## Julia\n\n::: {#4 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nx = range(0, 2π, length = 40)                             # 40 uniformly spaced samples\ny = sin.(x)                                               # the values at the samples\ni1 = convolution_interpolation(x, y; derivative = -1)     # the integral from x[1] = 0\ni2 = convolution_interpolation(x, y; derivative = -2)     # the twofold integral, also anchored at 0\n\na, b = 1.0, 2.5                                           # the limits of a definite integral\ni1(1.0), i2(1.0), i1(b) - i1(a)                           # 1 − cos(1), 1 − sin(1), cos(a) − cos(b)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(0.45969769434630703, 0.15852901543032516, 1.3414459211930763)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Python\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom convinterp import convolution_interpolation\n\nx = np.linspace(0.0, 2 * np.pi, 40)                       # 40 uniformly spaced samples\ny = np.sin(x)                                             # the values at the samples\ni1 = convolution_interpolation(x, y, derivative=-1)       # the integral from x[0] = 0\ni2 = convolution_interpolation(x, y, derivative=-2)       # the twofold integral, also anchored at 0\n\na, b = 1.0, 2.5                                           # the limits of a definite integral\ni1(1.0), i2(1.0), i1(b) - i1(a)                           # 1 − cos(1), 1 − sin(1), cos(a) − cos(b)\n```\n\n:::\n\n| | ∫₀¹ f | ∫₀¹∫₀ˢ f | ∫ₐᵇ f |\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|\n| interpolant | 0.459697694346 | 0.158529015430 | 1.341445921193 |\n| exact | 0.459697694132 | 0.158529015192 | 1.341445921415 |\n\n\n\n## Integrals don't cost convergence order\n\nWhere each derivative order costs one order of convergence, integrals cost none: the integral\nof the interpolant converges at least as fast as the interpolant itself. Runge's function with\n`:b7`, the interpolant and its first and second integrals:\n\n::: {#8 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code code-fold=\"true\" code-summary=\"The convergence study\"}\nrunge(x) = 1 / (1 + 25x^2)                                # Runge's function\nirunge = (x -> (atan(5x) + atan(5)) / 5,                  # its integral from −1\n          x -> (x * (atan(5x) + atan(5)) - log(1 + 25x^2) / 10 + log(26) / 10) / 5)  # its twofold integral from −1\nxr = range(-1, 1, length = 2001)                          # fine points for measuring the error\nns = [41, 81, 161, 321, 641, 1281]                        # the numbers of samples\nhs = 2 ./ (ns .- 1)                                       # the corresponding spacings\norders = 0:-1:-2                                          # the interpolant and two integrals\nexact = (runge, irunge...)                                # the function and its integrals\n\nerrors = [begin\n              xn = range(-1, 1, length = n)\n              itp_m = convolution_interpolation(xn, runge.(xn); kernel = :b7, derivative = m)\n              maximum(abs.(itp_m.(xr) .- exact[1-m].(xr)))\n          end for n in ns, m in orders]\n\nplot(xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10, xlabel = \"spacing h\", ylabel = \"largest error\",\n     legend = :bottomleft, xflip = true)\nfor (j, m) in enumerate(orders)\n    plot!(hs, errors[:, j], marker = :circle, label = m == 0 ? \"interpolant\" : \"integral order $(-m)\")\nend\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n| | interpolant | integral order 1 | integral order 2 |\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|\n| observed convergence order | 7.12 | 8.00 | 7.82 |\n\n\n\nEvaluating an integral costs about the same as evaluating the interpolant, whatever the number\nof knots: the contribution of every knot left of the stencil is a polynomial in the position\nwithin the cell, and those are precomputed when the interpolant is built. Each kernel supports\nintegrals up to a fixed order, 2 for `:a0` and `:a1`, 4 for the other `:a` kernels, 6 for\n`:b5` and 8 for `:b7` to `:b13`; the precomputed tables grow with the order, so high orders in\nseveral dimensions cost memory.\n\n## Integrals in more dimensions\n\nIn more dimensions the orders are given per axis, and integrals combine freely with\ninterpolation and derivatives along the other axes. The antiderivative along every axis is the\niterated integral from the corner of the grid; a derivative along one axis and an integral\nalong another is a Leibniz-rule operator, in one evaluation:\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## Julia\n\n::: {#12 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nxg = range(0, 2π, length = 60)                            # knots along x\nyg = range(0, 2π, length = 60)                            # knots along y\ndata = [sin(xi) * cos(yi) for xi in xg, yi in yg]         # sin(x)·cos(y) on the grid\n\nixy = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data; derivative = (-1, -1))   # ∫∫ from (0, 0)\nix  = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data; derivative = (-1, 0))    # ∫ dx, interpolated in y\ndxiy = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data; derivative = (1, -1))   # ∂/∂x ∫ dy\n\nixy(1.0, 2.0), ix(1.0, 2.0), dxiy(1.0, 2.0)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(0.4180019303973203, -0.19130174119961763, 0.49129549731347355)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Python\n\n```python\nxg = np.linspace(0.0, 2 * np.pi, 60)                      # knots along x\nyg = np.linspace(0.0, 2 * np.pi, 60)                      # knots along y\ndata = np.sin(xg)[:, None] * np.cos(yg)[None, :]          # sin(x)·cos(y) on the grid\n\nixy = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data, derivative=(-1, -1))    # ∫∫ from (0, 0)\nix  = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data, derivative=(-1, 0))     # ∫ dx, interpolated in y\ndxiy = convolution_interpolation((xg, yg), data, derivative=(1, -1))    # ∂/∂x ∫ dy\n\nixy(1.0, 2.0), ix(1.0, 2.0), dxiy(1.0, 2.0)\n```\n\n:::\n\n| at (1, 2) | ∫₀¹∫₀² f | ∫₀¹ f dx | ∂/∂x ∫₀² f dy |\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|\n| interpolant | 0.418001930397 | -0.191301741200 | 0.491295497313 |\n| exact | 0.418001930392 | -0.191301741181 | 0.491295496434 |\n\n\n\n## Discontinuous data\n\nThe `:a0` kernel, which is nearest-neighbour interpolation, integrates piecewise-constant data\nexactly: a spike one cell wide and $1/h$ high integrates to a unit step, which is how a\nsampled impulse should behave.\n\n::: {#16 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nn = 1001                                                  # knots\nxs = range(0, 2π, length = n)\nh = step(xs)                                              # the spacing\nspike = [abs(xi - π) < h / 2 ? 1 / h : 0.0 for xi in xs]  # a unit-area spike at π\nF = convolution_interpolation(xs, spike; kernel = :a0, derivative = -1)   # its integral\n\nxf = range(0, 2π, length = 801)\nplot(xf, F.(xf), label = \"∫ spike\", xlabel = \"x\")\nplot!(xf, (xf .> π) .* 1.0, color = :black, linestyle = :dash, label = \"unit step\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" />\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Nonuniform grids\n\nIntegrals are available on uniform grids only, in both packages; the nonuniform kernels of the\nJulia package support interpolation and derivatives but not antiderivatives. The\n[nonuniform page](nonuniform.qmd) says what to do instead.\n\n",
